\chapter{Quick Start}
\label{ch:quickstart}

This chapter walks through a complete literate program from a blank file
to a running binary and a typeset document. It uses C for concreteness,
but nothing here is C-specific.

\section{Get weft}

If you have the source tree, build the binary once:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
make            # tangle the .weft sources and compile ./weft
./weft --help   # sanity check: prints usage and a directives summary
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\noindent That leaves a \verb|weft| executable in the current directory.
Put it on your \verb|PATH| if you like.

\section{Write your first \texttt{.weft}}

Create \verb|hello.weft|. A weft file is mostly ordinary prose (\LaTeX\ or
Markdown); the only special tokens are the \emph{at-commands}. Type this
in verbatim:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{listings}
\usepackage{color}
\begin{document}

We are going to greet the world. The greeting itself is a small
reusable fragment, so the main routine reads like a sentence:

@@d Say hello @@{
printf("hello, %s\n", name);
@@}

The program picks a name and hands it to that greeting:

@@o hello.c @@{
#include <stdio.h>
int main(void) {
    const char *name = "world";
    @@<Say hello@@>
    return 0;
}
@@}

\end{document}
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

Three commands do all the work: \verb|@@d| \emph{defines} a named
fragment, \verb|@@o| \emph{opens} an output file, and
\hbox{\tt @@<name@@>} \emph{references} a fragment so its code is spliced
in where the reference sits. The \verb|@@{...@@}| braces delimit the code
(a \emph{scrap}); everything outside them is prose.

\section{Tangle: get the code}

Run weft with no flags. That is the default job --- tangle:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
weft hello.weft
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\noindent weft writes \verb|hello.c|, assembling the file exactly as the
\verb|@@o hello.c| scrap describes, with \hbox{\tt @@<Say hello@@>}
replaced by its fragment body:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
#include <stdio.h>
int main(void) {
    const char *name = "world";
    printf("hello, %s\n", name);
    return 0;
}
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\noindent (weft also inserts \emph{section markers} --- small comments
that map each line back to \verb|hello.weft|. They are covered in
Chapter~\ref{ch:debug}; ignore them for now.) Compile and run it like any
C program:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
cc -o hello hello.c && ./hello
hello, world
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\section{Weave: get the document}

Now ask for the document with \verb|-w|:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
weft -w tex hello.weft      # produces hello.tex
pdflatex hello.tex          # produces hello.pdf
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\noindent The PDF shows your prose with the code set beneath each
heading: \hbox{$\langle$\,\textit{Say hello}\,$\rangle$} and
\hbox{$\langle$\,\texttt{hello.c}\,$\rangle$} as linked headers, syntax
highlighted, with automatic ``used in'' / ``defines'' cross-references. To
resolve the scrap numbers you run weft once more after \LaTeX\ (weft reads
them back from the \verb|.aux| file):

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
weft -w tex hello.weft && pdflatex hello.tex
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

Prefer Markdown? Swap the format --- no \TeX\ needed:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
weft -w md hello.weft        # produces hello.md, renders on GitHub
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\section{What just happened}

You wrote \emph{one} file and got two artifacts from it:

\begin{quote}
\begin{tabular}{@@{}ll@@{}}
\verb|weft hello.weft|        & $\rightarrow$ \verb|hello.c| (tangle, the default) \\
\verb|weft -w tex hello.weft| & $\rightarrow$ \verb|hello.tex| $\rightarrow$ PDF (weave) \\
\verb|weft -w md hello.weft|  & $\rightarrow$ \verb|hello.md| (weave) \\
\end{tabular}
\end{quote}

\noindent That is the whole loop. From here: keep the Cheatsheet
(Chapter~\ref{ch:cheatsheet}) next to you, learn the full command set in
Chapter~\ref{ch:language}, and see bigger programs in
Chapter~\ref{ch:examples}.
